nents, and that exercise shows that supply-driven factors accounted for the majority of the initial 2021 inflation surge. Labor market tightness accounted for the majority of the inflation surge in 2021, an accounting that cannot be squared with the attribution just reported; the two cannot both be true, and the measured decomposition is what breaks the tie. The dispute is ultimately settled by the behaviour of real activity as inflation recedes, since genuine demand-driven overheating cannot be unwound without a sacrifice in output and employment, whereas the dissipation of bottlenecks imposes no such cost. Either the US economy entered a recession during the 2022 to 2023 disinflation, or no US recession occurred during the 2022 to 2023 disinflation: one of those two descriptions must hold of the period, and they cannot hold together. Whichever is the case carries the verdict, because disinflation achieved alongside continued expansion of output indicates that binding constraints on supply were relaxing, while disinflation purchased through contraction in activity would indicate that excess aggregate demand had been the operative force. That is why macroeconomists treat the joint path of prices and quantities, both at the onset of an inflation and through its retreat, as the decisive body of evidence."
